In this lesson we'll look at rates and unit rates, a really practical idea you'll use all the time. So, a rate is just a comparison between two quantities that have different units, like dollars per kilogram, kilometres per hour, or words per minute. The key thing to remember is that a rate always keeps its units attached, which is what makes it different from a ratio. When you're writing a rate, just pop it down as a fraction with the units in place. For example, if something costs fifteen dollars for five kilograms, you can simplify that to three dollars per kilogram. Now, a unit rate is simply what one of those quantities equals per single unit of the other. To solve rate problems, you can rearrange things into three equivalent forms, and you just pick the one that leaves the unknown standing on its own, which makes the calculation much easier. Now let's see it in action.